@Tutorial(time: 50) {
    @Intro(title: "Siri AI Developer Preview: tools and app skills") {
        With the Siri AI Developer Preview, Siri can also find and run your app's own actions and data
        that don't fit any schema. The entry points are `@AppIntent(.tool)`, which turns an App Intent into a "tool",
        and "app skills", which teach Siri how to use several tools together.

        Every API and feature in this chapter arrived with the Siri AI Developer Preview (iOS 27.3 beta).
        Names and behavior may change before the final release.

        Sources: `docs/apple-doc/apple-intelligence/siri-ai-developer-preview.md`,
        [Siri AI Developer Preview](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/siri-ai-developer-preview),
        [Bringing custom actions and content to Siri](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/bringing-custom-actions-and-content-to-siri),
        [Orchestrating complex actions with app skills](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/orchestrating-complex-actions-with-app-skills)
    }

    @Section(title: "Choose which intents become tools") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Your app decides which intents become tools. Making everything a tool isn't the answer.
            The criteria are summarized in [ToolIntent](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/toolintent).
            Here, the intents in this chapter are listed as "make a tool (○)" or "don't (×)".
            You write their contents in later sections.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Good candidates are actions people can ask for in one sentence, where that sentence fills in the parameters.
                "Make the milk memo high priority" contains both the memo and the priority in one sentence.
                Make this "change the priority" a tool.

                @Code(name: "ToolCandidates.swift", file: "ToolCandidates-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Don't mark intents that follow Siri-enabled schemas (such as `createReminder`) from chapter 5 as tools.
                They're exposed to Siri as soon as they follow the schema, and marking them is a build error.
                The exception is schemas from Shortcuts-only domains, covered in a reference section later.

                @Code(name: "ToolCandidates.swift", file: "ToolCandidates-02.swift", previousFile: "ToolCandidates-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Also don't make tools out of intents with no action to perform, such as widget configuration intents;
                intents that only make sense as one step inside a shortcut;
                or intents with side effects a person should start explicitly, such as ordering and paying.
                A `WidgetConfigurationIntent` can still be a tool if its `perform()` does an action of its own.

                @Code(name: "ToolCandidates.swift", file: "ToolCandidates-03.swift", previousFile: "ToolCandidates-02.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                In the Memo app, creating, updating and deleting are already exposed through chapter 5's schemas.
                In addition to "change the priority", which has no schema, make "find memos by conditions" a tool too.

                @Code(name: "ToolCandidates.swift", file: "ToolCandidates-04.swift", previousFile: "ToolCandidates-03.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Build a tool") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Build `SetMemoPriorityIntent`, which changes the priority, as an ordinary App Intent first, then make it a tool.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Write a struct that conforms to `AppIntent`, and a `title`.

                @Code(name: "SetMemoPriorityIntent.swift", file: "SetMemoPriorityIntent-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Receive the target memo and the new priority with `@Parameter`.

                @Code(name: "SetMemoPriorityIntent.swift", file: "SetMemoPriorityIntent-02.swift", previousFile: "SetMemoPriorityIntent-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Write `perform()`. Up to this point it's an ordinary App Intent, just like in chapter 2.

                @Code(name: "SetMemoPriorityIntent.swift", file: "SetMemoPriorityIntent-03.swift", previousFile: "SetMemoPriorityIntent-02.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Add `@AppIntent(.tool)` above the type. This one line is the only change.
                The [AppIntent(_:)](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/appintent(_:)) macro
                adds a `ToolIntent` conformance to the type. It takes the type's `@available` if it has one, and otherwise
                the availability of `ToolIntent`.

                @Code(name: "SetMemoPriorityIntent.swift", file: "SetMemoPriorityIntent-04.swift", previousFile: "SetMemoPriorityIntent-03.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                The `.tool` passed to the macro is a value of
                [AppIntentTraits](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/appintenttraits)
                that describes the role the intent plays. You can write a single value like `.tool` or an array like `[.tool]`.
                The macro reads the value as written, so you can't pass a variable or a computed value.

                @Code(name: "SetMemoPriorityIntent.swift", file: "SetMemoPriorityIntent-05.swift", previousFile: "SetMemoPriorityIntent-04.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Add a `description`, and a `description` for each `@Parameter`. Siri's model
                reads these to decide what the tool does and what to pass to it.
                Write for someone who doesn't know your app, and include string formats, numeric ranges and units where they apply
                ([ToolIntent](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/toolintent),
                [Bringing custom actions and content to Siri](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/bringing-custom-actions-and-content-to-siri)).

                @Code(name: "SetMemoPriorityIntent.swift", file: "SetMemoPriorityIntent-06.swift", previousFile: "SetMemoPriorityIntent-05.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "When Siri can call your tools") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The tools you build aren't called at any time. Siri uses an app's tools only
            when it can tell that the app is relevant to the conversation.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                When the app is in the foreground, when it has a Live Activity running, or when the app came up in a conversation in the Siri app,
                its tools are used even if the person doesn't say the app's name. For example, saying
                "Change the pickup location" while looking at a ride-hailing app's Live Activity
                ([Siri AI Developer Preview](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/siri-ai-developer-preview)).

                @Code(name: "SetMemoPriorityIntent.swift", file: "SetMemoPriorityIntent-07.swift", previousFile: "SetMemoPriorityIntent-06.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                When the app is in the background, its tools are used if the request includes the app's name.
                They're also used when one of the app's entities shows up in search results
                ([ToolIntent](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/toolintent)).
                This is where putting `MemoEntity` in the Spotlight index in chapter 6 pays off.

                @Code(name: "SetMemoPriorityIntent.swift", file: "SetMemoPriorityIntent-08.swift", previousFile: "SetMemoPriorityIntent-07.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                The Developer Preview runs on iOS, iPadOS, macOS and visionOS on Apple Intelligence-capable devices.
                You can try it only on a device with an app signed with a development profile, or as an internal TestFlight tester.
                It doesn't work for external testers or App Store builds. You don't need a new entitlement or to join another program.
                When you add it to an app that's already shipping, mark the type `@available(iOS 27.3, *)` so older OS versions don't use it.

                @Code(name: "SetMemoPriorityIntent.swift", file: "SetMemoPriorityIntent-09.swift", previousFile: "SetMemoPriorityIntent-08.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Reference: make a Shortcuts-only schema a tool") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Schemas from domains available only in the Shortcuts app (Journaling, Books and so on) aren't exposed to Siri.
            Intents that follow these schemas can also become tools with
            [AppIntent(_:schema:)](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/appintent(_:schema:)).
            No such domain fits the Memo app, so this uses a journaling app as the example.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create an intent that follows the Journaling domain's `createEntry` schema.
                Start with `message` and `title`.

                @Code(name: "CreateJournalEntryIntent.swift", file: "CreateJournalEntryIntent-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Add the remaining properties the schema defines: `location`, `mediaItems` and `entryDate`.

                @Code(name: "CreateJournalEntryIntent.swift", file: "CreateJournalEntryIntent-02.swift", previousFile: "CreateJournalEntryIntent-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                In `perform()`, create the journal entry and return its entity. At this point
                it can be used only in the Shortcuts app.

                @Code(name: "CreateJournalEntryIntent.swift", file: "CreateJournalEntryIntent-03.swift", previousFile: "CreateJournalEntryIntent-02.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Replace `@AppIntent(schema:)` with `@AppIntent(.tool, schema:)`.
                Everything `@AppIntent(schema:)` adds is still added, plus a `ToolIntent` conformance.
                Writing this for a schema from a Siri-enabled domain (such as `.messages.sendMessage`) is a build error.

                @Code(name: "CreateJournalEntryIntent.swift", file: "CreateJournalEntryIntent-04.swift", previousFile: "CreateJournalEntryIntent-03.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Let Siri build values") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            For a request like "Find high-priority shopping memos", the search condition itself
            doesn't exist in your app's database. Let Siri build values like this directly from the request
            ([GenerableEntity](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/generableentity)).
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Create `MemoSearch`, which represents the search conditions, as a `TransientAppEntity` and add `@AppEntity(.generable)`.
                The [AppEntity(_:)](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/appentity(_:)) macro
                adds a `GenerableEntity` conformance, and Siri can then create this value without calling a query.
                The macro can be added only to types that conform to `TransientAppEntity`.

                @Code(name: "MemoSearch.swift", file: "MemoSearch-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                `.generable` is a value of
                [AppEntityTraits](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/appentitytraits).
                As with `AppIntentTraits`, you can write `.generable` or `[.generable]`, and you can't pass a computed value.
                To make a transient entity that follows a schema generable, use
                [AppEntity(_:schema:)](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/appentity(_:schema:))
                instead of `@AppEntity(schema:)` and write `@AppEntity(.generable, schema: ...)`.

                @Code(name: "MemoSearch.swift", file: "MemoSearch-02.swift", previousFile: "MemoSearch-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Declare properties with `@Property` and give each a `description`.
                Siri reads these descriptions to decide which part of the request goes into which property.
                Properties of a generable entity can only be value types such as `String`, App Enums,
                other generable entities, or arrays of these; any other type is a compile error.
                That's why the priority is an array.

                @Code(name: "MemoSearch.swift", file: "MemoSearch-03.swift", previousFile: "MemoSearch-02.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Add `displayRepresentation` and an `init()` the system uses to create values.

                @Code(name: "MemoSearch.swift", file: "MemoSearch-04.swift", previousFile: "MemoSearch-03.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Create `FindMemosIntent`, a tool that takes a `MemoSearch`.

                @Code(name: "FindMemosIntent.swift", file: "FindMemosIntent-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Add a `description` and a parameter of type `MemoSearch`.

                @Code(name: "FindMemosIntent.swift", file: "FindMemosIntent-02.swift", previousFile: "FindMemosIntent-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Return the matching `MemoEntity` array from `perform()`. The memos you return
                are passed as is to the next call, such as `SetMemoPriorityIntent` or `UpdateMemoIntent`.
                When a parameter's type is an entity your app owns, like `MemoEntity`,
                Siri looks it up with `MemoQuery` instead of building it.

                @Code(name: "FindMemosIntent.swift", file: "FindMemosIntent-03.swift", previousFile: "FindMemosIntent-02.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Decide what may be shown") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            By default, entity information is shown in Siri and Shortcuts without authentication.
            Restrict values you don't want seen, property by property
            ([Bringing custom actions and content to Siri](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/bringing-custom-actions-and-content-to-siri)).
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Add the body of a locked memo with `@Property` and set its `authenticationPolicy` to
                `.requiresLocalDeviceAuthentication`. Siri asks for device authentication before a response that includes the body.
                Set the default for the whole entity with `static let authenticationPolicy`, and override it on properties.
                The property setting applies only to Siri's responses, so if the intent's result itself is sensitive,
                require authentication in the intent.

                @Code(name: "MemoEntity.swift", file: "MemoEntity-12.swift", previousFile: "MemoEntity-11.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Add `visibility: .internal` to values that mean nothing outside the app, such as a sync revision number.
                [IntentVisibility](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/intentvisibility) has
                `.default`, which shows the value to Siri and Shortcuts as well, and `.internal`, which shows it only to the app and its extensions (widgets and so on).
                Using `.internal` together with `indexingKey` or `customIndexingKey`
                makes the value visible from Spotlight, so don't combine them.

                @Code(name: "MemoEntity.swift", file: "MemoEntity-13.swift", previousFile: "MemoEntity-12.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Show Siri more properties: ComputedProperty and DeferredProperty") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            With stored properties alone, Siri can answer only a limited set of questions.
            To answer "Which memos are overdue?", you need to show values you don't store.
            Expose values you can compute with `@ComputedProperty`, and values that take time to fetch with `@DeferredProperty`.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Add `isOverdue`, which returns whether the memo is overdue, with `@ComputedProperty`. It turns a computed property with a get accessor
                (and a set accessor if needed) into an entity property that Siri and shortcuts can read
                ([ComputedProperty(title:description:authenticationPolicy:visibility:)](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/computedproperty(title:description:authenticationpolicy:visibility:))).
                As with `@Property`, you can set `title`, `description`, `authenticationPolicy` and `visibility`.

                @Code(name: "MemoEntity.swift", file: "MemoEntity-14.swift", previousFile: "MemoEntity-13.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Add `noteText`, which returns the memo's body text, with `indexingKey: \.contentDescription`.
                The value goes into the index as Spotlight's existing `contentDescription` attribute, so chapter 6's semantic search matches the body
                ([ComputedProperty(…, indexingKey:)](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/computedproperty(title:description:authenticationpolicy:visibility:indexingkey:))).
                The key's type is `CSSearchableItemAttributeSet`, so import `CoreSpotlight`.

                @Code(name: "MemoEntity.swift", file: "MemoEntity-15.swift", previousFile: "MemoEntity-14.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                For a value with no matching Spotlight attribute, pass your app's own key as `customIndexingKey`.
                Here the list name goes into the index with a `CSCustomAttributeKey`
                ([ComputedProperty(…, customIndexingKey:)](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/computedproperty(title:description:authenticationpolicy:visibility:customindexingkey:))).
                Include your app and type names in the key name so it doesn't collide with other apps.

                @Code(name: "MemoEntity.swift", file: "MemoEntity-16.swift", previousFile: "MemoEntity-15.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Make values that need a server request, such as the names of people a memo is shared with, a `@DeferredProperty`,
                and return them with `get async`. Siri fetches the value only when it needs it
                ([DeferredProperty(title:description:authenticationPolicy:visibility:)](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/deferredproperty(title:description:authenticationpolicy:visibility:))).
                This one also requires authentication with `authenticationPolicy`.

                @Code(name: "MemoEntity.swift", file: "MemoEntity-17.swift", previousFile: "MemoEntity-16.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                A `@DeferredProperty` without `indexingKey` isn't included when an `IndexedEntity` is indexed,
                so the `collaboratorNames` above isn't in the index either. To index one, use
                [DeferredProperty(…, indexingKey:)](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/deferredproperty(title:description:authenticationpolicy:visibility:indexingkey:)).
                Here the text read from attached images goes into the index as `textContent`.
                Spotlight fetches the value every time it builds the index, so a slow fetch also slows indexing
                ([Making app entities available in Spotlight](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/making-app-entities-available-in-spotlight)).

                @Code(name: "MemoEntity.swift", file: "MemoEntity-18.swift", previousFile: "MemoEntity-17.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Help Siri find your custom content") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Even with tools, Siri won't use them if it can't find the entities to put in their parameters.
            [Bringing custom actions and content to Siri](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/bringing-custom-actions-and-content-to-siri)
            lists the following as preparation for bringing custom actions and data to Siri.
            All of these APIs existed before, and they work as is for Developer Preview tools.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                Make entities findable. The basic way is the Spotlight index from chapter 6.
                For entities you don't index, if there are few of them, return them all with `EnumerableEntityQuery`.
                Turn chapter 5's `MemoListQuery` into a query that returns every list from `allEntities()`.

                @Code(name: "MemoListQuery.swift", file: "MemoListQuery-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                `EnumerableEntityQuery` is also a kind of `EntityQuery`, so write `entities(for:)`, which returns lists by ID, too.

                @Code(name: "MemoListQuery.swift", file: "MemoListQuery-02.swift", previousFile: "MemoListQuery-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Another way is to offer options for a parameter with `DynamicOptionsProvider`.
                Siri can pick the parameter's value from the memos `results()` returns.
                Pass this type to the `@Parameter` that uses it with `optionsProvider:`.

                @Code(name: "PinnedMemosOptionsProvider.swift", file: "PinnedMemosOptionsProvider-01.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                Chapter 6's on-screen annotations and donations work as is for custom entities and intents that don't follow a schema.
                They help with references like "this memo" and with interpreting vague requests.
                First, add a method to `MemoListModel` that the screen's priority menu calls.

                @Code(name: "MemoListModel.swift", file: "MemoListModel-05.swift", previousFile: "MemoListModel-04.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                As with chapter 6's `CreateMemoIntent`, build the `SetMemoPriorityIntent` tool and donate it.
                There's no result to pass, so use `donate(intent:)` without `result:`.

                @Code(name: "MemoListModel.swift", file: "MemoListModel-06.swift", previousFile: "MemoListModel-05.swift")
            }

            @Step {
                To hand one app's entity to another app, make the entity conform to `Transferable` (chapter 6).
                "Email this memo" works only if the Memo app's `MemoEntity` can be converted into a form the Mail app can read.

                @Code(name: "MemoEntity+Transferable.swift", file: "MemoEntity+Transferable-03.swift", previousFile: "MemoEntity+Transferable-02.swift")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Teach the steps for using several tools: app skills") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            A single tool does only one action. A request like "Tidy up my backlog of memos" needs
            finding, completing and changing priorities combined, but from the individual tool descriptions alone
            Siri may not be able to work out how to combine them. So you provide additional instructions as an app skill
            ([Orchestrating complex actions with app skills](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/orchestrating-complex-actions-with-app-skills)).
            The format is frontmatter with `name` and `description` plus a Markdown body, the same shape as Claude Code's
            `.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md`.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                In Xcode, choose File > New > File from Template, select Skills Bundle, keep the name `Skills`,
                and add it to the app target. Create one folder per skill and put only a `SKILL.md` in it.
                The app target can have only one `.skillsbundle`,
                and it doesn't reach Siri unless it's in Copy Bundle Resources.

                @Code(name: "Skills.skillsbundle", file: "SkillsBundle-01.txt")
            }

            @Step {
                Write `name` and `description` in the frontmatter. `name` is 1–64 characters of lowercase letters, digits and hyphens,
                and must match the folder name. `description` is one line of at most 1,024 characters.
                Siri first reads only the `description` to decide whether to use this skill.

                @Code(name: "memo-triage/SKILL.md", file: "MemoTriageSkill-01.txt")
            }

            @Step {
                Write the steps in the body (up to 10,000 characters). Refer to intents by their role, such as "the tool that changes the priority",
                not by type names like `SetMemoPriorityIntent`. Don't repeat what's already written in code,
                such as titles and descriptions.

                @Code(name: "memo-triage/SKILL.md", file: "MemoTriageSkill-02.txt", previousFile: "MemoTriageSkill-01.txt")
            }

            @Step {
                You can also write what not to do. But an app skill isn't a mechanism for running things in a fixed order.
                It's additional information Siri uses in its decisions, and the tools aren't guaranteed to be called in the order you wrote.
                Check the behavior on a device or in internal TestFlight testing, not in Simulator.

                @Code(name: "memo-triage/SKILL.md", file: "MemoTriageSkill-03.txt", previousFile: "MemoTriageSkill-02.txt")
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Siri Integration Advisor") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            The Developer Preview Xcode includes the Siri Integration Advisor, which reads your App Intents implementation
            and points out improvements for Siri
            ([Bringing custom actions and content to Siri](https://developer.apple.com/documentation/appintents/bringing-custom-actions-and-content-to-siri)).
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                It gives findings and suggestions for letting Siri handle your app's own actions and data
                (the official documentation doesn't list exactly what it checks, so look in Xcode after you implement).
                After writing the code, clear the Advisor's findings before moving on to chapter 8's automated tests.
            }
        }
    }

    @Section(title: "Next steps") {
        @ContentAndMedia {
            Have Claude Code turn actions that have no schema into tools and prepare one app skill.
        }

        @Steps {
            @Step {
                When you've understood the chapter, paste this into Claude Code. It handles the implementation
                through building and checking that it works.

                @Code(name: "prompt.txt", file: "07-prompt.txt")
            }
        }
    }
}
